# FutureVera – Notes (TryHackMe)

Started with a basic scan using `nmap -sC -sV -T4 <IP>`. Found SSH and a web server running on 80/443.

Added the domain to `/etc/hosts`:
`10.146.157.118 futurevera.thm`

Did some quick directory enumeration with gobuster. Found `/assets`, `/css`, `/js`. Looked into them, especially the JS file (`scripts.js`), but it was just template code — nothing useful.

At that point I got stuck for a bit.

Then I went back to the challenge description and realized it literally says this is about **subdomain enumeration**.

Switched approach and ran vhost enumeration with gobuster. That gave me:

* `sum.futurevera.thm`
* `sumsum.futurevera.thm`

Added both to `/etc/hosts`.

The support subdomain didn’t expose anything obvious. No inputs, no directories, nothing to interact with. That usually means you need to dig deeper instead of brute forcing more paths.

Next step was checking the SSL certificate using openssl. That revealed another hidden subdomain:

`sumsumsumsum.support.futurevera.thm`

Added it to `/etc/hosts` and accessed it. Same situation again — clean page, nothing obvious.

Eventually checked HTTP headers instead of the page content using:
`curl --head`

That’s where the flag showed up — inside a redirect:

`Location: http://flag{xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx}...`

---

## Takeaways

* Always read the challenge description carefully
* Subdomain enumeration goes beyond brute forcing (certificates matter)
* If a page looks too clean, check headers and redirects
* Don’t tunnel vision on one technique — switch approach when stuck

